# What Railway must not upload.
#
# == WHY THIS EXISTS ALONGSIDE .dockerignore ===================================
#
# Two different steps read two different files. .dockerignore governs what goes into
# the image once the build starts; .railwayignore governs what gets UPLOADED to
# Railway in the first place.
#
# Without this file, `railway up` tars the entire working tree -- which here is about
# 1.2 GB of Kenney art, a Unity Library/ cache and the whole of .git, none of which the
# Dockerfile so much as looks at. That is a slow upload on every deploy, for nothing.
#
# == KEEP IN STEP WITH .dockerignore ===========================================
#
# Every rule below is also in .dockerignore, and SecurityChecks asserts that. They can
# legitimately diverge -- the upload could exclude more than the build does -- but a
# rule that exists only in the build is a file being uploaded for no reason, and a rule
# that exists only here is a file the build expects and cannot find.
#
# What the Dockerfile actually needs is small: server/, Assets/Scripts/Rules/ and
# Assets/StreamingAssets/.

**/bin/
**/obj/
Tools/verify/build/
Library/
Temp/
Logs/
UserSettings/
obj/
*.csproj.user
*.sln
